@style(IDocDemo)
@title(Charts: Comparing amounts)

Bars, stacked bars, waterfalls and funnels: one number per thing, side by side. Each chart is followed by the source that drew it, and every example carries its own data.

@table(widths=equal)
@row
@cell
@b{Bar}

One number per category. The default when you have labels and values.

@graph(type=bar, x=field, y=Bachelor, title="Bachelor's degrees awarded")
@data
field,Bachelor,Master,Doctorate
Engineering,126000,52000,11000
Computer science,104000,48000,2300
Biology,131000,15000,8100
Physics,9600,2100,1900
@end

@code(idoc)
@graph(type=bar, x=field, y=Bachelor, title="Bachelor's degrees awarded")
@data
field,Bachelor,Master,Doctorate
Engineering,126000,52000,11000
Computer science,104000,48000,2300
Biology,131000,15000,8100
Physics,9600,2100,1900
@end
@end
@endcell
@cell
@b{Your own colours}

colors= sets the colours in order — hex or CSS names. Any chart takes it; colormap= picks a named scale instead.

@graph(type=bar, x=team, y=points, colors=#cc0033;#5f6a72;#f0a202, title="colors= takes hex or names")
@data
team,points
Scarlet,120
Grey,90
Gold,60
@end

@code(idoc)
@graph(type=bar, x=team, y=points, colors=#cc0033;#5f6a72;#f0a202, title="colors= takes hex or names")
@data
team,points
Scarlet,120
Grey,90
Gold,60
@end
@end
@endcell
@endtable

@table(widths=equal)
@row
@cell
@b{Grouped bar}

Several series side by side — compare categories AND series at once.

@graph(type=bar, x=field, y=Bachelor;Master;Doctorate, title="Degrees by level")
@data
field,Bachelor,Master,Doctorate
Engineering,126000,52000,11000
Computer science,104000,48000,2300
Biology,131000,15000,8100
Physics,9600,2100,1900
@end

@code(idoc)
@graph(type=bar, x=field, y=Bachelor;Master;Doctorate, title="Degrees by level")
@data
field,Bachelor,Master,Doctorate
Engineering,126000,52000,11000
Computer science,104000,48000,2300
Biology,131000,15000,8100
Physics,9600,2100,1900
@end
@end

Naming more than one y column groups them. This is the default multi-series bar layout.
@endcell
@cell
@b{Stacked bar}

Series stacked into one bar — the total AND its parts, together.

@graph(type=stacked_bar, x=year, y=Public;Private, title="US secondary schools")
@data
year,Public,Private
2009,98817,33366
2011,98328,30861
2013,98271,33619
2015,98277,34576
2017,98469,32461
2019,98469,30492
@end

@code(idoc)
@graph(type=stacked_bar, x=year, y=Public;Private, title="US secondary schools")
@data
year,Public,Private
2009,98817,33366
2011,98328,30861
2013,98271,33619
2015,98277,34576
2017,98469,32461
2019,98469,30492
@end
@end
@endcell
@endtable

@table(widths=equal)
@row
@cell
@b{Waterfall}

How a starting number becomes an ending one, step by step.

@graph(type=waterfall, x=item, y=delta, title="Budget")
@data
item,delta
Opening,120
Salaries,-45
Equipment,-18
Grant,30
@end

@code(idoc)
@graph(type=waterfall, x=item, y=delta, title="Budget")
@data
item,delta
Opening,120
Salaries,-45
Equipment,-18
Grant,30
@end
@end
@endcell
@cell
@b{Funnel}

A quantity narrowing through stages.

@graph(type=funnel, x=stage, y=n, title="Applications")
@data
stage,n
Applied,1200
Screened,540
Interviewed,160
Offered,44
@end

@code(idoc)
@graph(type=funnel, x=stage, y=n, title="Applications")
@data
stage,n
Applied,1200
Screened,540
Interviewed,160
Offered,44
@end
@end
@endcell
@endtable
